{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it","id":1540436,"title":"Attributes and Skill Dots Mastery Level : How do you handle it?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-08-15T09:30:33-07:00","id":1540436,"title":"Attributes and Skill Dots Mastery Level : How do you handle it?","content":"I love chronicles attributes and skills system. I think since they revised all the attributes in nWoD that they really managed to create a very solid structure. The way Power/Finesse/Resistance are crossed with Mental/Physical/Social is so elegant that I reused it in my homebrew system. I find it strikes the perfect balance of complex and simple in a neat and organized fashion.<br /> <br /> Something I\u2019ve always had trouble with, in CoD, however, is the way each dot implies a level of mastery that\u2019s almost exponential.<ul><li>One dot in an attribute means it\u2019s a weakness, in a skill it means entry-level mastery.</li> <li>Two dots is average for an attribute, or professional level for a skill.</li> <li>Three dots is above average for attributes, or experienced for a skill.</li> <li>Four dots is extremely gifted or an expert in a skill.</li> <li>Five dots is human peak in both attributes and skills.</li> </ul>And it\u2019s not super hard to increase a character\u2019s attribute and skill to 5.<br /> This often means that, unless the storyteller restricts character creation and advancement, that a party will be composed of a weird setup of overly exceptional characters. I\u2019ve had parties with the equivalent of a world class surgeon, a spec Ops sniper, Bruce Lee, Einstein and a billionaire within the span of a few games.<br /> <br /> And while it is certainly fun to play such characters and I appreciate that the system lets you create them, sometimes it makes very little sense to have all these exceptionally gifted characters in the same chronicles.<br /> <br /> I can\u2019t blame the players, though. If you want to excel at something you put points into the skill you want to use that make sense. I also find that compared to other Chronicles lines, the basic CoD doesn\u2019t have a lot of things you can spend XP one. Sure there are a lot of interesting merits, but once you got what you need, you don\u2019t have disciplines, power stats and covenants status to increase.<br /> <br /> So how do you usually handle it? Do you restrict attributes and skills to a range that makes more sense? Do you redefine what dots means in the context of your chronicles? Or you just enjoy the campiness of it all?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-15T11:45:17-07:00","id":1540443,"title":"","content":"I don't put too much stock into the fluff descriptions of what each dot level means. In actual gameplay the mechanics do not bear this out. In fact, mechanically dots work the opposite way. Dice pools have sharp diminishing returns. The second dot in an attribute affects the odds of success far more than the fourth or fifth dot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-15T11:59:48-07:00","id":1540444,"title":"","content":"I'll get the big point out of the way and just note that the limits on Beat earnings means it's unlikely one will earn more than one Experience per session, which puts a bit of a throttle on how quickly one can raise Attributes as the second most expensive Trait available to a bluebook mortal.<br /> <br /> That said, considering Merits are where the entire extended social circle mechanics live (organization rules from Geist, Mummy, and Deviant notwithstanding), and considering that Area of Expertise and Interdisciplinary Specialty are deliberately priced to make Specialties more efficient than Skill dots, while it's theoretically possible for players to decide they want to go a month at a time without getting anything new for their character there's a wealth of opportunities to apply pressure from fresh directions given the breadth of options for Social Merits alone.<br /> <br /> &quot;Once you've got what you need&quot; is a tricky phrase to start bandying about in a setting where the supernatural is in play even if you don't break into things like Dyads or Supernatural Merits, because even at its least fantastical Chronicles is operating in a mode adjacent to a Stephen King story. Mere inertia isn't likely to justify a character becoming an Olympic athlete or a Nobel Prize winning scientist, but if the character is putting the establishing effort in onscreen to explain where those purchases come from I don't see much issue with PCs reaching the peak of human potential after years of dodging monsters and running the rat race.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-15T12:37:01-07:00","id":1540462,"title":"","content":"To build off that point:<br /> <br /> Think about the IT book/movies. The adult Losers Club members have a number of highly accomplished people. Bev is a major fashion designer, Richie is a nationally famous shock-jock or comedian depending on the version, Ben is a rich architect, and Bill is a novelist/screenwriter modeled off Stephen King in terms of level of success. Stan and Eddie are both in far less glamorous jobs as adults, but are presented in at least mid-management levels of financial success and have more functional adult families. Mike is the only real exception, but he stayed in Derry.<br /> <br /> When it comes to dealing with Pennywise/It though... all that success in the mortal world doesn't amount to much. <br /> <br /> Ultimately, a lot of it comes down to Session Zero style talks with your players. If you want a specific tone and ask them all to make more well rounded and less specialized characters.. most reasonable players will go with it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-15T13:29:12-07:00","id":1540465,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540443#post1540443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't put too much stock into the fluff descriptions of what each dot level means.</div> </div> </div> </div>It\u2019s pretty much how I decided to handle it too. High dots in attributes and skills mean being good in the context of the chronicles and is peak to what characters can do, but I try not to dwell too much on it outside its scope.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540443#post1540443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> In actual gameplay the mechanics do not bear this out. In fact, mechanically dots work the opposite way. Dice pools have sharp diminishing returns. The second dot in an attribute affects the odds of success far more than the fourth or fifth dot.</div> </div> </div> </div>You\u2019re right as far as CoD go, extra successes rarely mean anything outside of combat.<br /> <br /> Having a high dice pool can make a long-term task trivial, but that\u2019s something else completely.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540444#post1540444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll get the big point out of the way and just note that the limits on Beat earnings means it's unlikely one will earn more than one Experience per session, which puts a bit of a throttle on how quickly one can raise Attributes as the second most expensive Trait available to a bluebook mortal.</div> </div> </div> </div>That\u2019s fair, I tend to be pretty generous as far as beats go and end out experience a bit faster than what the book suggests. However, it\u2019s possible to have a starting character with maxed out dice pool nonetheless.<br /> <br /> I could have sworn there was a restriction on the fifth dot of attributes or skill at character creation, but I can't seem to find it. It must have been a house rule I implemented a long time ago.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540444#post1540444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> That said, considering Merits are where the entire extended social circle mechanics live (organization rules from Geist, Mummy, and Deviant notwithstanding), and considering that Area of Expertise and Interdisciplinary Specialty are deliberately priced to make Specialties more efficient than Skill dots, while it's theoretically possible for players to decide they want to go a month at a time without getting anything new for their character there's a wealth of opportunities to apply pressure from fresh directions given the breadth of options for Social Merits alone.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, but that is very campaign dependent. I will admit that I tend to run chronicles on a smaller scale so a lot of social merits tend not to be super useful or downright disruptive with status, allies and resources being the prime culprit. If I'm running a monster hunt through the wood and a character has '' Status 5 (Army) '', there was clearly something wrong with me I did when approving that character sheet.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540444#post1540444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> &quot;Once you've got what you need&quot; is a tricky phrase to start bandying about in a setting where the supernatural is in play even if you don't break into things like Dyads or Supernatural Merits, because even at its least fantastical Chronicles is operating in a mode adjacent to a Stephen King story. Mere inertia isn't likely to justify a character becoming an Olympic athlete or a Nobel Prize winning scientist, but if the character is putting the establishing effort in onscreen to explain where those purchases come from I don't see much issue with PCs reaching the peak of human potential after years of dodging monsters and running the rat race.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also fair, but then again flavour is free. If it is required of a character to be an Olympic athlete to get athletic at five dots, then you can make them an Olympic athlete.<br /> <br /> Of course, the storyteller can say no, that\u2019s also fair.<br /> <br /> Chronicles can sometimes be in a weird conceptual spot in the sense that it\u2019s a mystery horror game, in which character can be super-competent badasses. Then again, as you mentioned if you keep dodging monsters, you\u2019re bound to become good at it\u2026 until you don\u2019t!<br /> <br /> That\u2019s not a problem that\u2019s inherent to CoD however, I think it\u2019s inherent to point buy systems that allow you to reach such height, the storyteller has to set the tone in game 0. CoD is also fairly tame as far as power scaling goes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540462#post1540462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> To build off that point:<br /> Think about the IT book/movies.</div> </div> </div> </div>I will shamefully admit that I\u2019m a very bad book reader as I have a lot of trouble making mental images on my own, which tend to make book reading tedious for me. I should probably give the movie a chance, though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540462#post1540462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Bev is a major fashion designer, Richie is a nationally famous shock-jock or comedian depending on the version, Ben is a rich architect, and Bill is a novelist/screenwriter modeled off Stephen King in terms of level of success. Stan and Eddie are both in far less glamorous jobs as adults, but are presented in at least mid-management levels of financial success and have more functional adult families. Mike is the only real exception, but he stayed in Derry.</div> </div> </div> </div>That\u2019s fair. It might have to do with the fact that I\u2019m more easily inspired with chronicles that deal with more \u2018\u2019regular folk\u2019\u2019 than international stars.<br /> <br /> The player I had the original discussion with mentioned that his character was getting too much point at character creation and he didn\u2019t quite know what to do with it! It\u2019s kind of hard to make an inexperienced character with the amount of skill point you get, your character is bound to be at least experienced in something, or know a bunch of stuff. I'll probably homebrew a character creation that's fully experienced based eventually. That will probably help in that regard.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540462#post1540462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> When it comes to dealing with Pennywise/It though... all that success in the mortal world doesn't amount to much.</div> </div> </div> </div>Since I haven\u2019t read/watch the story and all I know about it is the second-word accounts, I\u2019ll take your words for granted, but I\u2019d wager it probably have more to do with the fact that none of the mentioned character seems particularly well equipped to deal with Eldritch Entities.And again, that\u2019s fine, some skills are next to worthless in some chronicles but can lead to great character.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540462#post1540462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Ultimately, a lot of it comes down to Session Zero style talks with your players. If you want a specific tone and ask them all to make more well rounded and less specialized characters.. most reasonable players will go with it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Absolutely. I was honestly more interested in how people handled it or if they did at all.<br /> Thanks for the replies \ud83d\ude0a<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-15T14:26:12-07:00","id":1540472,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540465#post1540465\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I could have sworn there was a restriction on the fifth dot of attributes or skill at character creation, but I can't seem to find it. It must have been a house rule I implemented a long time ago.</div> </div> </div> </div>1e had the fifth dot of an Attribute or Skill cost double at character creation, but Trait price scaling worked differently in 1e and one of 2e's design mandates was to not have normal things cost differently at character creation versus with advancement.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-15T17:51:21-07:00","id":1540483,"title":"","content":"The problem is partly with point-buy systems, which solves itself in Session 0, but partly because the game is <b>not </b>about average people dealing with the supernatural.<br /> <br /> Most RPGs, CoD is no exception, work under the premise that the PCs are exceptional people. They may be mundanely exceptional, but are exceptional nonetheless.<br /> <br /> It's just a result of them being protagonists, and being expected to face and survive situations most people can't deal with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":". 08-15-2024, 08:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-16T02:29:17-07:00","id":1540503,"title":"","content":"Honestly, I find the idea that it's difficult to make a non-exceptional (stat-wise) PC kinda strange.<br /> <br /> Example:<br /> <br /> Two Skills at 3 (your character is experienced but not an expert at a job that has a few facets to it).<br /> Four Skills at 2 (Supplementary training to your job, significant hobbies, etc.)<br /> Eight Skills at 1 (Most people pick up the basics of lots of things even if they never get very far with them)<br /> Ten Skills at 0 (Lots of stuff your character has no training in at all, and lots of room for horizontal growth over time via XPs).<br /> <br /> Starting with a Skill at 4 or 5 doesn't force you to be cripplingly overspecialized, sure, but it's not a small investment of your 22 starting Skill dots. The spread I posted above is not unreasonable for an extremely accomplished student in higher education, or most working adults","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-16T02:49:19-07:00","id":1540505,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540443#post1540443\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">. In fact, mechanically dots work the opposite way. Dice pools have sharp diminishing returns. The second dot in an attribute affects the odds of success far more than the fourth or fifth dot.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is this not technically wrong.<br /> <br /> Like yes, increased strength does have diminishing returns when looking at opening jars. You either succeed at opening the jar or you fail... More success don't actually matter.<br /> <br /> Fine, you are right, but you if ore all the cases where the task has a narrative based negative modifier. If it is a -4 task, most ppl are now down to a chance die, but experts still have a high chance of success? Is that not how the system rewards high stat characters?<br /> <br /> In the coverse, if your game is not focused on those high difficulty tasks, you players are not optomising for the game, and would be better off increase another attribute to 3 rather than a single attribute to 5.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-16T07:57:46-07:00","id":1540508,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540483#post1540483\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem is partly with point-buy systems, which solves itself in Session 0, but partly because the game is <b>not </b>about average people dealing with the supernatural.<br /> <br /> Most RPGs, CoD is no exception, work under the premise that the PCs are exceptional people. They may be mundanely exceptional, but are exceptional nonetheless.<br /> <br /> It's just a result of them being protagonists, and being expected to face and survive situations most people can't deal with.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh indeed, most RPGs will bring the focus on exceptional protagonists as they deal with stories and adventure that are exceptional in themselves.<br /> While I do prefer my chronicle games on the more\u2026 relatable scale, I\u2019m fully aware that facing monsters, spirits and vampires has nothing to do with mundane life.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540503#post1540503\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, I find the idea that it's difficult to make a non-exceptional (stat-wise) PC kinda strange.<br /> <br /> Example:<br /> <br /> Two Skills at 3 (your character is experienced but not an expert at a job that has a few facets to it).<br /> Four Skills at 2 (Supplementary training to your job, significant hobbies, etc.)<br /> Eight Skills at 1 (Most people pick up the basics of lots of things even if they never get very far with them)<br /> Ten Skills at 0 (Lots of stuff your character has no training in at all, and lots of room for horizontal growth over time via XPs).<br /> <br /> Starting with a Skill at 4 or 5 doesn't force you to be cripplingly overspecialized, sure, but it's not a small investment of your 22 starting Skill dots. The spread I posted above is not unreasonable for an extremely accomplished student in higher education, or most working adults</div> </div> </div> </div>Originally when I wrote the first draft of this post, I had the preconception that making a mundane PC was hard until I tested it and arrived at the same conclusion you did; it\u2019s entirely possible to create a non-exceptional character using a non-exceptional spread.<br /> <br /> What my player and I found harder was creating inexperienced PC without making them exceptional.<br /> <br /> 3 dots implies a level of expertise that a working adult should reach after a few years in their domain. If we look at Academic, Cod gives the example of having a PhD, or being a college teacher; at 3 dots of athletics, a character could be an NHL player; persuasion 3 is a talented salesman that can win multiple awards; 3 dots of medicine is appropriate for a surgeon, etc.<br /> <br /> 2 dots imply professional training. At 2 dots of persuasion a character could be a lawyer, 2 dots of medicine means they can work as a nurse; 2 dots of empathy mean you could work as a therapist, etc.<br /> <br /> 1 dot is where I\u2019d probably put most hobby skills or natural talent. At 1 dot of survival, a character is a regular wilderness camper or hiker; at 1 dot of empathy, they can pick up vibes pretty easily; at 1 dot of firearms, they spend time at the range and know how firearms work; 1 dot of athletics implies going to the gym, play sport regularly, etc.<br /> <br /> For an inexperienced character I\u2019d probably want to avoid putting more than a 2 in a few skills and avoid putting a 3, unless they are very gifted in a particular area, meaning they kind of become exceptional.<br /> <br /> If we try to make a character with no skills at 3 dots and go with four skills at 2, we are left with 12s hobby or novice skills. While they excel at nothing, the character is able to pool 3 to 4 dice in 16 out of 24 skills. To me, that\u2019s quite exceptional.<br /> <br /> Hell, I know RPGs are not made to replicate reality, but I don\u2019t think I\u2019d be able to fill all the point skills if I tried to make a character based on the skill set that I have even assuming we treat specialties as general skill. I certainly have no skill above 2 and I\u2019m 30. To be fair, I\u2019m far from exceptional.<br /> <br /> You said it yourself, the spread you shared, which is also what I\u2019d go with for a typical \u2018\u2019non-exceptional\u2019\u2019 character, is appropriate for a working adult with experience or a very accomplished student.<br /> <br /> As others said, though, it\u2019s fine, all systems have their assumption, and CoD seems to assume characters are going to be coming from experienced and competent background. I certainly wouldn\u2019t go into DnD hoping to play a regular townsfolk with no heroic abilities.<br /> <br /> In the end, I think reinterpreting what those skill dots means in the context if my chronicles will yield to more satisfying games as I won\u2019t have to fight against the system. The resource merit certainly does so, I don\u2019t see why I couldn\u2019t do the same with skill.<br /> <br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-16T12:57:19-07:00","id":1540526,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540508#post1540508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What my player and I found harder was creating inexperienced PC without making them exceptional.<br /> <br /> 3 dots implies a level of expertise that a working adult should reach after a few years in their domain. If we look at Academic, Cod gives the example of having a PhD, or being a college teacher; at 3 dots of athletics, a character could be an NHL player; persuasion 3 is a talented salesman that can win multiple awards; 3 dots of medicine is appropriate for a surgeon, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel like you're skipping over some nuance in the book.<br /> <br /> The game does not, at all care about how your character comes about their level of Skill. &quot;Being a voracious reader&quot; is also an example of Academics 3. The game draws no distinction between innate aptitude, hobby and informal learning, formal education, or on-the-job experience.<br /> <br /> Yes, there's always going to be a point where a character is too inexperienced for the default starting level of a game to make sense. But a nurse that's been on the job for 20 years can have higher Medicine than 2 dots without being &quot;exceptional&quot; as the book defines things. You're not really fighting the system, your fighting your interpretation of the system that - and sorry if this is a bit blunt - feels overly reductive. You seem to be treating &quot;Athletics 3 = can play in a major sports league&quot; as &quot;if a character has Athletics 3 and isn't a professional athlete it's exceptional,&quot; which really isn't in the text.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-16T17:55:23-07:00","id":1540536,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540526#post1540526\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> You're not really fighting the system, your fighting your interpretation of the system that - and sorry if this is a bit blunt - feels overly reductive.</div> </div> </div> </div>No offense taken; we\u2019re only talking here. You're absolutely free not to agree with my interpretation. \ud83d\ude0a<br /> <br /> What I mean by I\u2019m \u2018\u2019not going to be fighting against the system\u2019\u2019 is that I won\u2019t argue with mechanics when I perceive an issue with how flavor is presented. Flavor can easily be adjusted. The system assumes character can reach 5 in their attributes and 5 in their skills and I see no mechanical reason not to allow characters to attain those heights, then I\u2019ll play with that in mind and reflavor to what I feel is appropriate for the chronicles. Which is only one of the solutions. Running a story that takes into account exceptional character is also another one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540526#post1540526\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> You seem to be treating &quot;Athletics 3 = can play in a major sports league&quot; as &quot;if a character has Athletics 3 and isn't a professional athlete it's exceptional,&quot; which really isn't in the text.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, of course, a character can have 3 dots of athletics even though they aren\u2019t a major league player or professional sports player, that\u2019s why I used the word \u2018\u2019imply\u2019\u2019 multiple time. The game implies a certain level of proficiency at each differing levels of mastery.<br /> <br /> But, if a character has as much skill dots as a professional league player then they are, no matter how we look at it, they then they are as good as a major league player. How they acquired the skill is of little importance. The hobbyist with 3 dots is going to be as skilled as a professional player with 3 dots. To me, using the examples in the book, a character having 3 dots in a skill they aren\u2019t even using as a profession seems to imply either a great dedication or natural talent, which is in itself kind of exceptional.<br /> <br /> To reiterate my point, I think it\u2019s perfectly feasible to make a non-exceptional character, as you demonstrated, the experienced nurse is a very good example, what is harder, as you acknowledged, is to make an inexperienced character. The game does give out a lot of skills at character creation and if a player wants to play a younger inexperienced character with nothing exceptional, then it\u2019s a bit tough to do.<br /> <br /> Other point buy systems handle this by allowing player to place your starting character point, however, you want (Gurps comes to mind, which I\u2019m not a big fan of but that\u2019s beside the point), but chronicles do not; you get A 5/4/3 spread in your attribute and an 11/7/4 spread in your skills, like it or not.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m not saying that\u2019s necessarily a fault of the system nor that it should be fixed. If the game assumes a certain level of competency from its characters, then those characters probably must have that general level of competency to participate meaningfully. No system I\u2019ve experienced completely escapes from this.<br /> <br /> If I had to homebrew a solution, it would be that I would allow characters to be built fully on an experience budget from character creation with some general rules that would dictate minimum experience and maximum experience in each category.<br /> <br /> Just for fun, because I enjoy doing this, here\u2019s how I\u2019d do it.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Assuming integrity starts at 7 and cannot be modified at creation, A typical starting CoD character is.<ul><li>48 XP of experience in attributes. With a 20/16/12 split between mental/physical/social.</li> <li>44 XP in skill points + 3 XP in specialties. With a 22/14/8 split between mental/physical/social.</li> <li>7 XP into Merits.</li> </ul>For a total of 102 XP.<br /> <br /> Going point buy, A character would need to spend a total 102 XP of experience:<ul><li>32 to 52 XP in Attributes.With no one of the 3 Categories under 8 and over 20 XP.</li> <li>30 to 60 points in Skills &amp; Specialties. (No restriction here, i never quite understood why the 11/7/4 split exists.)</li> <li>7 Points or more in merits.</li> </ul>This would certainly allow easier power gaming, but also greater character creation customization.<br /> Even using this as an homebrew option, that should remain totally optional for players feeling that it would complicate character creation too much.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Anyway, I\u2019m drifting the thread quite a bit, apologies.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-16T19:05:38-07:00","id":1540541,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540508#post1540508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh indeed, most RPGs will bring the focus on exceptional protagonists as they deal with stories and adventure that are exceptional in themselves.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>That's not it, and I think the difference is important here.<br /> <br /> It's not just that they deal with exceptional stories, but that as protagonists they're generally more capable of dealing with the coming challenges than the average person, even if they had no prior notion of how good they are.<br /> <br /> But I think your problem has another, simpler solution. And it isn't to re-skin the dots as representing less aptitude. I actually think this is a bad approach, because you'll need to increase the stats of your NPCs to compensate, and that can get out of hand.<br /> <br /> Why don't you simply give the PCs less starting points? That's all you need if you want them to be inexperienced youngsters.<br /> <br /> You're right that the game as in the core book isn't meant for this type of character. But you're wrong that it needs to start with that much points. There's even an official 1st Ed book about playing with children and teenagers.<br /> <br /> All you need to run a game with less dots is to pay more attention to Specialties and Equipment bonus. You can even increase starting Specialties and Merits, but reduce everything else.<br /> <br /> Characters with less dots simply rely more on having the right tools and look for advantageous conditions, while the standard character leans more on being self reliant. An Att 2 + Skill 1 character with good tools is easily on the 5 or 6 dice ballpark.<br /> <br /> Let's see: Attributes 4/3/2, 15 Skill points, 5 Specialties, 12 Merits. Change as much as you like, this isn't going to make the game impossible for your players.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-16T19:46:28-07:00","id":1540546,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540541#post1540541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's not it, and I think the difference is important here.<br /> <br /> It's not just that they deal with exceptional stories, but that as protagonists they're generally more capable of dealing with the coming challenges than the average person, even if they had no prior notion of how good they are.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed, I couldn\u2019t have formulated it better.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540541#post1540541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But I think your problem has another, simpler solution. And it isn't to re-skin the dots as representing less aptitude. I actually think this is a bad approach, because you'll need to increase the stats of your NPCs to compensate, and that can get out of hand.</div> </div> </div> </div>It has its weaknesses, however, when I meant in the context of the chronicle, i also mean taking NPCs skills into account. NPCs aren\u2019t magically going to develop a higher cap. I guess it can get weird once the scope of the campaign shift and players start dealing with Word Class skilled NPCs and their \u2018\u2019mundane 5 dot\u2019\u2019 suddenly now mean they are on part with the best in the world.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540541#post1540541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why don't you simply give the PCs less starting points? That's all you need if you want them to be inexperienced youngsters.<br /> <br /> You're right that the game as in the core book isn't meant for this type of character. But you're wrong that it needs to start with that much points. There's even an official 1st Ed book about playing with children and teenagers.<br /> <br /> All you need to run a game with less dots is to pay more attention to Specialties and Equipment bonus. You can even increase starting Specialties and Merits, but reduce everything else.</div> </div> </div> </div>Unthinkable my players would riot! :P<br /> <br /> But more seriously, my issue isn\u2019t necessarily with the amount of points character gets. It\u2019s that some concepts are very hard to do right. Especially since characters get an abundance of skill points at creation and very little merits, which are, in my opinions, the main tools players have to give their character a unique identity.<br /> <br /> But that\u2019s no where near a deal breaker for me, I\u2019m fine with homebrewing. I originally made that post to learn other\u2019s perspective on the matter.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540541#post1540541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Characters with less dots simply rely more on having the right tools and look for advantageous conditions, while the standard character leans more on being self reliant. An Att 2 + Skill 1 character with good tools is easily on the 5 or 6 dice ballpark.</div> </div> </div> </div>The point about tools is certainly interesting. We tend not to use tool too much, I hadn\u2019t thought of it, good point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540541#post1540541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Let's see: Attributes 4/3/2, 15 Skill points, 5 Specialties, 12 Merits. Change as much as you like, this isn't going to make the game impossible for your players.</div> </div> </div> </div>Certainly, an interesting distribution for younger/inexperienced character. What do you think of going full point buy as I detailed in the spoilers of my previous reply?</span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-17T01:02:30-07:00","id":1540553,"title":"","content":"Moving to all XP really doesn't change much. It's a bit more customizable, but it doesn't really address the default competence that the CofD starting spread uses. Less points in Skills just means more points elsewhere. A bunch of PCs running around with that XP shift to high Attributes and low Skills is probably going to make them feel more competent, not less.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-17T07:53:45-07:00","id":1540571,"title":"","content":"In my experience the variety of challenges that the narrator puts in the characters way is the key point.<br /> In my first chronicle i had lot of player overspecialized (one of them with Empathy 5 and Brawling 5 and not much else), i ve proposed lots of situations in wich many different skills would be useful, and the most specialized player were very efficient in certain situations, but in many more others they were not even capable at helping with teamwork.<br /> i assume that those situation had bored them, and now at chronicle number 3 all the players have spontaneusly spreaded the skills points more evenly, becoming more capable to solve different situations.<br /> Some of them still reach the top 5 in skills during play, but not more than in one skill, and it seems more like a personal growth caused by a series of exceptional and dangerous challenges.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","name":"Neos01","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1021"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-17T09:02:53-07:00","id":1540574,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540553#post1540553\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Moving to all XP really doesn't change much. It's a bit more customizable, but it doesn't really address the default competence that the CofD starting spread uses. Less points in Skills just means more points elsewhere. A bunch of PCs running around with that XP shift to high Attributes and low Skills is probably going to make them feel more competent, not less.</div> </div> </div> </div>With my proposed ''buy point character creation'', a character could start with as little as 8 dots of attributes instead of the normal 12, 13 points of skills instead of 22, four specialties instead of 3. That would leave them with 40 points of merits. I'm not sure if those numbers are right or not, but I'd say it's a lot more than a bit more customizable, but we can disagree on definition.<br /> <br /> If PCs decided to invest in attributes and merits rather than skills, then it could mean they have a lot of raw untapped potential but little actual training. One could make the classic sheltered psychic character with a lot of supernatural powers and little real word skills.<br /> <br /> Not saying those aren't exceptional PCs, which again, just to be sure I'm clear enough, i have nothing against.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540553#post1540553\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my experience the variety of challenges that the narrator puts in the characters way is the key point.</div> </div> </div> </div>Absolutely. Fighting skills are going to be as useful as the importance of combat in the chronicles. Session 0 also goes a long way into encouraging players to invest in ''the right skills'', a chronicle with characters that aren't fit to progress the story forwards is very hard to run right.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540553#post1540553\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my first chronicle i had lot of player overspecialized (one of them with Empathy 5 and Brawling 5 and not much else), i ve proposed lots of situations in wich many different skills would be useful, and the most specialized player were very efficient in certain situations, but in many more others they were not even capable at helping with teamwork.<br /> i assume that those situation had bored them, and now at chronicle number 3 all the players have spontaneusly spreaded the skills points more evenly, becoming more capable to solve different situations.</div> </div> </div> </div>Baring some character concept, my players tend to specialize in one or two things and invest a few dots in useful secondary skills that every character need.<br /> <br /> Athletics, Brawl, Firearms, Stealth, Empathy + one other social skill; those are all skills any character is probably best to have in a generic multifaceted chronicle, while others can be handled by the party specialist. Do you really need more than 1 character to have dots in any mental skill, unless it's the campaign focus? I guess canceling the -3 untrained penalty can be useful.<br /> <br /> I guess it really depends on how we run our chronicles, but in mine, players tend to let the party specialist do their thing and they focus on theirs; it makes the group stronger as a whole.<br /> Then again, i agreed with your point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540553#post1540553\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Some of them still reach the top 5 in skills during play, but not more than in one skill, and it seems more like a personal growth caused by a series of exceptional and dangerous challenges.</div> </div> </div> </div>I really have nothing against character reaching high skill mastery as a result of encountering supernatural creatures, we're already out of the bound of reality as soon as the supernatural is involved.<br /> <br /> If i wasn't clear enough, my original inquiry was more along the line of ''How do you handle parties with characters that are so skilled they are a collection of World Class specialists in their field, <i>narratively</i>. ''.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-17T10:17:58-07:00","id":1540578,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540574#post1540574\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Do you really need more than 1 character to have dots in any mental skill, unless it's the campaign focus? I guess canceling the -3 untrained penalty can be useful.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Sometimes, not often, one player with like 3 dots in a mental skill had to do a research, build a thing (a protective amulet for example) or simply perform a medical care roll to downgrade aggravated with an extended action, and feared of failing (with 6 rolls and a target number of 10 it happened).<br /> Other players with 1 or 2 dots in the same skill had started to use often the teamwork rule to support the main player in those tasks.<br /> It seems like they enjoyed this mechanic","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","name":"Neos01","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1021"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-17T10:47:55-07:00","id":1540579,"title":"","content":"Ah yes, good point, i had forgotten that teamwork award extra die for each extra success i played too much V5 recently and teamwork simply award an extra die or two. Still, fairly niche, but nice to have. Really depends on the campaign i guess. <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Segoe UI Emoji\">\ud83d\ude0a</span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-17T13:17:54-07:00","id":1540588,"title":"","content":"To be fair if your character is a supernatural being of some type, he or she is going to be above average or outstanding in certain areas and what you envision what your character is and if the skills and attributes make sense such as an outgoing character should have at least a 3 or 4 in charisma and some supplemental social skills and maybe if it helps a real life person you know or see on the tv that gives you an idea of what direction you should go with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-18T11:44:37-07:00","id":1540642,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540546#post1540546\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">What do you think of going full point buy as I detailed in the spoilers of my previous reply?</span></div> </div> </div> </div>I second <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/\">Heavy Arms</a>, it doesn't by itself solves nothing, even if it doesn't harm things, either.<br /> <br /> What may help you to achieve your goal is to move points from Attributes and Skills to Merits. Your XP buy system can help make sure this transition conserves value, but it is lowering the points and caps for starting values what will make what you want.<br /> <br /> On how I deal with it narratively... I let world-class PCs be world-class characters. If the player chose to put 5 in something, I just parse with them what that means, if they do want it, so be it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-18T16:28:46-07:00","id":1540665,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540588#post1540588\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be fair if your character is a supernatural being of some type, he or she is going to be above average or outstanding in certain areas and what you envision what your character is and if the skills and attributes make sense such as an outgoing character should have at least a 3 or 4 in charisma and some supplemental social skills and maybe if it helps a real life person you know or see on the tv that gives you an idea of what direction you should go with.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, of course, I don't find the idea that a supernatural character having extraordinary attribute to be remotely troublesome. When I run a vampire Chronicles, I fully expected my PCs to have superhuman abilities, it\u2019s baked into the genre and the system.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540642#post1540642\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I second <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/\">Heavy Arms</a>, it doesn't by itself solves nothing, even if it doesn't harm things, either.<br /> <br /> What may help you to achieve your goal is to move points from Attributes and Skills to Merits. Your XP buy system can help make sure this transition conserves value, but it is lowering the points and caps for starting values what will make what you want.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough, when I want to run a lower powered chronicle, it stands to reason to reduce the starting value of characters. I\u2019ll have to test the point buy system anyway. I\u2019ve always found the Chronicles of Darkness character creation guidelines to be a bit too restrictive for my tastes. Point buy coupled with a lower starting XP value might solve my problem entirely.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1540436-attributes-and-skill-dots-mastery-level-how-do-you-handle-it?p=1540642#post1540642\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On how I deal with it narratively... I let world-class PCs be world-class characters. If the player chose to put 5 in something, I just parse with them what that means, if they do want it, so be it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough, that\u2019s also a perfectly valid way to handle things.<br /> <br /> Thanks for the input <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><span style=\"font-family:Segoe UI Emoji\">\ud83d\ude0a</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}